File: inference_concurrency_engine.support
Classification: INTERNAL — TRUSTED ENGINEERS ONLY
System: Ardhanarishvara Infrastructure Layer
Module: Inference Concurrency & VRAM Locking Engine (Y‑Prime Edition)
1. Overview
The Inference Concurrency & VRAM Locking Engine is the GPU traffic controller for the entire Ardhanarishvara ecosystem. It ensures that:

only one heavy inference job runs at a time

VRAM usage never exceeds safe limits

modules do not deadlock the GPU

multi‑agent concurrency does not crash the system

all AI entities (Arka, Astra, Aruhan, Arkastra, Lux, Disturition) share GPU resources safely

This engine is mandatory for any local LLM ecosystem running multiple autonomous modules.

It integrates with:

System Orchestrator

Orchestration Guardrails Engine

IPC Message Broker

Context Compaction Engine

Stability & Recovery Engine

2. Purpose
The engine solves three critical problems:

A. GPU Overload Prevention
Local LLMs can easily exceed VRAM limits.
This engine enforces a strict VRAM budget.

B. Concurrency Control
Multiple modules may attempt inference simultaneously.
This engine enforces a mutex lock to serialize access.

C. Queue Management
Modules enter a FIFO queue when waiting for GPU access.

This prevents:

race conditions

GPU thrashing

inference starvation

system freezes

3. Core Components
1. Lock Mechanism
A non‑blocking mutex that ensures only one module can run inference at a time.

2. VRAM Budget Manager
Tracks:

total VRAM

used VRAM

per‑request VRAM cost

Prevents over‑allocation.

3. Request Queue
FIFO structure storing:

module ID

VRAM cost

timestamp

4. Active Session Tracker
Stores the module currently holding the GPU lock.

5. Safety Reset System
Force‑clear mechanism to recover from:

deadlocks

stuck locks

corrupted queue states

6. Logging System
Tracks:

active module

queue length

VRAM usage

timestamps

Used for debugging and stability analysis.

4. Execution Pipeline
The engine follows a deterministic sequence:

Module requests access

Request enters queue

Engine checks:

lock availability

VRAM availability

If safe → lock granted

Module performs inference

Module releases lock

VRAM is freed

Next request is evaluated

This pipeline ensures predictable, safe GPU usage.

5. Locking Rules
Rule 1 — One Active Module
Only one module may hold the lock at any time.

Rule 2 — VRAM Must Be Available
If VRAM usage + request cost > budget → request denied.

Rule 3 — FIFO Queue
Requests are processed in order of arrival.

Rule 4 — No Forced Preemption
Active modules must release voluntarily unless a safety reset is triggered.

Rule 5 — Safety Reset Conditions
Triggered when:

lock is stuck

VRAM usage desyncs

queue grows beyond safe limits

orchestrator issues a kill signal

6. Integration Points
A. System Orchestrator
Receives lock status and decides:

when to retry

when to defer tasks

when to escalate to guardrails

B. Orchestration Guardrails Engine
Monitors for:

infinite inference loops

repeated lock failures

excessive queue buildup

C. IPC Message Broker
Broadcasts:

lock acquired

lock released

queue updates

VRAM warnings

D. Stability & Recovery Engine
Uses logs to detect:

drift spikes

overload patterns

inference bottlenecks

7. Debugging Entry Points
Safe to inspect:

active module

queue length

VRAM usage

history length

last release timestamp

Safe to modify:

VRAM budget

queue size limits

lock timeout thresholds

Do NOT modify:

mutex behavior

FIFO ordering

safety reset logic

VRAM accounting rules

8. Failure Modes & Recovery
1. Lock Stuck
Symptom: active module never releases.
Recovery: guardrails trigger force_clear.

2. VRAM Desync
Symptom: VRAM usage becomes negative or exceeds budget.
Recovery: reset VRAM counters + clear queue.

3. Queue Overflow
Symptom: too many modules waiting.
Recovery: orchestrator throttles module activity.

4. Deadlock Between Modules
Symptom: two modules wait on each other.
Recovery: guardrails kill both processes.

9. Expansion Guidelines
Engineers may safely:

add priority queues

add per‑module VRAM profiles

add inference timeouts

add multi‑GPU support

Engineers must NOT:

allow parallel inference

bypass VRAM checks

override lock enforcement

disable safety resets

10. Internal Philosophy
This engine is built on:

determinism

safety

fairness

resource discipline

predictable concurrency

It is not designed to maximize throughput — it is designed to prevent catastrophic failure in a multi‑agent local LLM ecosystem.

11. Notes for Trusted Engineers
Never bypass this engine for “quick tests.”

All inference‑capable modules must route through it.

VRAM budgets must match actual hardware.

Logs should be reviewed weekly during early deployment.
